{"term":"mend","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:639ee008c73a51f614","definition":"To physically repair (something that is broken, defaced, decayed, torn, or otherwise damaged).","example":"My trousers have a big rip in them and need mending.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["amend","correct","fix","fix up","furbish","retrieve","make whole","mend","patch","put to rights","rectify","recondition","reform","refurbish","remediate","remedy","renew","renovate","restore","restrive","revamp","right"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"transitive","wordFamily":["bellows to mend","hell mend someone","it's never too late to mend","least said, soonest mended","make and mend","make do and mend","mendable","mender","mendery","mend fences","mend one's pace","mend one's ways","mismend","on the mending hand","remend"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mend#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:30669cf8a66117260f","definition":"To add fuel to (a fire).","example":"[I]n, you Rogue, and vvipe the pigges, and mend the fire, that they fall not, or I'le both baſte and roaſt you, till your eyes drop out, like 'hem.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively, transitive","wordFamily":["bellows to mend","hell mend someone","it's never too late to mend","least said, soonest mended","make and mend","make do and mend","mendable","mender","mendery","mend fences","mend one's pace","mend one's ways","mismend","on the mending hand","remend"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mend#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:f808d986a41b7dd20b","definition":"To correct or put right (an error, a fault, etc.); to rectify, to remedy.","example":"Dro[mio of Syracuse]. […] [S]he ſvveats a man may goe ouer-ſhooes in the grime of it. / Anti[pholus of Syracuse]. That's a fault that vvater vvill mend.","pos":"verb","synonyms":["make good","set straight"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively, transitive","wordFamily":["bellows to mend","hell mend someone","it's never too late to mend","least said, soonest mended","make and mend","make do and mend","mendable","mender","mendery","mend fences","mend one's pace","mend one's ways","mismend","on the mending hand","remend"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mend#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:a85955ea3a1cd5205f","definition":"To put (something) in a better state; to ameliorate, to improve, to reform, to set right.","example":"Her stutter was mended by a speech therapist.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively, transitive","wordFamily":["bellows to mend","hell mend someone","it's never too late to mend","least said, soonest mended","make and mend","make do and mend","mendable","mender","mendery","mend fences","mend one's pace","mend one's ways","mismend","on the mending hand","remend"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mend#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:237ecbafeea4ebe289","definition":"To remove fault or sin from (someone, or their behaviour or character); to improve morally, to reform.","example":"Youle not endure him, god ſhall mend my ſoule, / Youle make a mutinie among my gueſts: […]","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively, transitive","wordFamily":["bellows to mend","hell mend someone","it's never too late to mend","least said, soonest mended","make and mend","make do and mend","mendable","mender","mendery","mend fences","mend one's pace","mend one's ways","mismend","on the mending hand","remend"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mend#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:e5ec2bc29a59d27128","definition":"In mend one's pace: to adjust (a pace or speed), especially to match that of someone or something else; also, to quicken or speed up (a pace).","example":"Cudgell thy braines no more about it, for your dull aſſe vvill not mend his pace vvith beating, […]","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively, transitive","wordFamily":["bellows to mend","hell mend someone","it's never too late to mend","least said, soonest mended","make and mend","make do and mend","mendable","mender","mendery","mend fences","mend one's pace","mend one's ways","mismend","on the mending hand","remend"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mend#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:30fe40506ee8b131cb","definition":"To correct or put right the defects, errors, or faults of (something); to amend, to emend, to fix.","example":"Salt Earth and bitter are not fit to ſovv, / Nor vvill be tam'd or mended vvith the Plough.","pos":"verb","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"archaic, figuratively","register":"archaic, figuratively, transitive","wordFamily":["bellows to mend","hell mend someone","it's never too late to mend","least said, soonest mended","make and mend","make do and mend","mendable","mender","mendery","mend fences","mend one's pace","mend one's ways","mismend","on the mending hand","remend"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mend#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:320befc8b9fd6690a3","definiti